# Syllabus keywords added to the glossary: (id, name, topics, definition, example[, options])
# Topic keys: pf, oop, mech, ssa, web, auto, sep, sdlc
#
# The optional sixth item is a dict:
#   'nesa': True    the definition is NESA's own, verbatim, from resources/se-syllabus-glossary.md
#                   (Software Engineering 11-12 Syllabus glossary, (c) NSW Education Standards Authority).
#                   The card gets a "NESA" badge and shows under the "NESA glossary" filter. If the term is
#                   already on the page, add-glossary-terms.py replaces its definition in place and adds the
#                   badge, keeping its tags, example and See also (pass None for the name and example then).
#   'see': [ids]    "See also" links under the card (added to an existing card if it has none of them).
# Definitions and examples may hold simple HTML such as <code>...</code>; write & < > as &amp; &lt; &gt;.
# A definition may have more than one paragraph (separate with a blank line) or end in a
# <ul class="term-def-list"> list, as NESA's "data types" does.
#
# TO ADD MORE TERMS (for example the page agents' reference-glossary/*.json candidates):
#   1. Add (id, name, [topic keys], definition, example) tuples to TERMS below. Ids are term-kebab-case and must
#      not already be on topics/glossary.html; check names too, and merge near-duplicates (SQL injection and
#      SQL Injection Attack are one term, an existing id such as term-sql-injection wins).
#   2. python3 scripts/add-glossary-terms.py && python3 scripts/build-glossary.py && python3 scripts/site-chrome.py
#      (site-chrome adds the topic icons to the new cards' tags).
#   3. python3 scripts/check-site.py && bash scripts/validate-alignment.sh
